import numpy as np
# Imports NumPy for mathematical and matrix operations.

# Define two fuzzy sets
A = np.array([0.2, 0.5, 0.8, 1.0])
# Creates the first fuzzy set with four membership values.

B = np.array([0.4, 0.6, 0.3, 0.7])
# Creates the second fuzzy set with four membership values.


# Mathematical operations on fuzzy sets

union = np.maximum(A, B)
# Union selects the maximum membership value from both sets.

intersection = np.minimum(A, B)
# Intersection selects the minimum membership value from both sets.

complement = 1 - A
# Complement subtracts each membership value of A from 1.

difference = np.minimum(A, 1 - B)
# Difference calculates the minimum between A and complement of B.


# Display fuzzy set operations

print("Fuzzy Set A:", A)
# Displays the first fuzzy set.

print("Fuzzy Set B:", B)
# Displays the second fuzzy set.

print("Union:", union)
# Displays the union of A and B.

print("Intersection:", intersection)
# Displays the intersection of A and B.

print("Complement of A:", complement)
# Displays the complement of A.

print("Difference A-B:", difference)
# Displays the difference between A and B.


# Cartesian product of two fuzzy sets

R = np.minimum.outer(A, B)
# Creates a fuzzy relation using the minimum of every pair of values.

print("\nCartesian Product Relation R:")
# Displays the heading for relation R.

print(R)
# Displays the first fuzzy relation.


# Define two more fuzzy sets

C = np.array([0.3, 0.7, 0.9])
# Creates the third fuzzy set.

D = np.array([0.5, 0.8])
# Creates the fourth fuzzy set.

S = np.minimum.outer(C, D)
# Creates the second fuzzy relation using Cartesian product.

print("\nCartesian Product Relation S:")
# Displays the heading for relation S.

print(S)
# Displays the second fuzzy relation.


# Max-Min Composition

composition = np.zeros((len(A), len(D)))
# Creates an empty matrix to store the composition result.

for i in range(len(A)):
# Goes through every row of the first relation.

    for j in range(len(D)):
    # Goes through every column of the second relation.

        values = []
        # Creates an empty list to store minimum values.

        for k in range(len(C)):
        # Goes through the common elements of both relations.

            values.append(min(R[i][k], S[k][j]))
            # Calculates the minimum value for each pair.

        composition[i][j] = max(values)
        # Selects the maximum among the calculated minimum values.

print("\nMax-Min Composition:")
# Displays the heading for the final result.

print(composition)
# Displays the final max-min composition matrix.


This program performs mathematical operations on fuzzy sets using Python. First, I created two fuzzy sets A and B containing membership values between 0 and 1. Then I performed four operations: union, intersection, complement, and difference. Union takes the maximum membership value, intersection takes the minimum, complement subtracts the membership value from 1, and difference calculates the minimum between A and the complement of B. After that, I created fuzzy relations using the Cartesian product of two fuzzy sets. The Cartesian product uses the minimum operation to generate a relation matrix. I created two relations, R and S. Finally, I performed max-min composition by taking the minimum of corresponding elements from the two relations and then selecting the maximum value. The final matrix represents the max-min composition of the two fuzzy relations.

Explanation of the output:
•	Union: Displays the maximum values from A and B.
•	Intersection: Displays the minimum values from A and B.
•	Complement: Displays 1 minus each value of A.
•	Difference: Displays the difference between the two fuzzy sets.
•	Cartesian product R and S: Displays the relation matrices created using the minimum operation.
•	Max-Min Composition: Displays the final matrix obtained by calculating minimum values first and maximum values afterward.
